// App-authored plain-English rendering of Arrian VII.9–10, based on E. J. Chinnock (1884).
export const alexanderOpis={
 id:'alexander-opis',title:'Speech at the mutiny at Opis',speaker:'Alexander the Great',date:'324 BC · preserved in Arrian, Anabasis VII.9–10',
 context:'At Opis, Macedonian soldiers protested Alexander’s plan to send older and wounded men home, amid resentment over the position of Persians in his army. Arrian describes arrests and orders for executions before this address. Reconciliation came later, after Alexander withdrew and elevated Persian officers; the speech alone did not immediately end the mutiny.',
 edition:'Plain-English rendering made for this app from E. J. Chinnock’s public-domain 1884 translation of Arrian VII.9–10. It follows the whole address, simplifying vocabulary and sentence structure. Arrian wrote centuries after Alexander; this is a speech in his historical account, not a contemporary transcript.',
 source:'https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Anabasis_of_Alexander/Book_VII/Chapter_IX',
 readerLabel:'Read the whole address in plain English',
 note:'The sections identify rhetorical functions, not an original six-part outline. The claims about conquest, generosity, and loyalty belong to the speaker as Arrian presents him. Device notes refer to this plain-English rendering. The source continues into Chapter X.',
 sections:[
  {part:0,title:'Let them leave—but make them answer for it',text:'“I am not speaking to stop you from going home. As far as I am concerned, you may go wherever you wish. But I want you to understand what you were before, and what you have become in our service. I will begin, as I should, with my father Philip.',explanation:'He appears to grant their request, then changes the subject from their right to leave to what they owe. The promised comparison supplies the route through his argument.',devices:[{name:'Grant the request, change its meaning · concession',quote:'you may go wherever you wish',mechanism:'Permission removes the immediate argument over whether they can leave. What follows makes leaving feel like a moral decision.',tryIt:'Acknowledge someone’s freedom to decline, then explain the responsibility you hope they will consider.'}]},
  {part:1,title:'Recall what Philip changed',text:'He found you wandering and poor, most of you wearing skins and tending a few sheep in the mountains. You struggled to protect them against the Illyrians, Triballians, and neighboring Thracians. He gave you cloaks instead of skins, brought you down to the plains, and made you able to face those neighbors in battle. You no longer had to rely on mountain strongholds for safety instead of your own courage.\n\nHe settled you in cities and gave you useful laws and customs. Instead of being subjects of the peoples who had plundered you and your property, you became their rulers. He added much of Thrace to Macedonia, secured important coastal towns, opened the country to trade, and made it safe to work the mines.\n\nHe made you rulers of the Thessalians, whom you once feared, and subdued the Phocians, opening a broad road into Greece where there had been a narrow and difficult one. Athens and Thebes had threatened Macedonia; he humbled them, with my help in the campaign. Instead of paying tribute to Athens and submitting to Thebes, you saw them depend on us for their safety.\n\nHe entered the Peloponnese and settled its affairs. He was publicly chosen to lead the rest of Greece against Persia, bringing honor not only to himself but to the Macedonian people.',explanation:'The before-and-after story casts the army’s present status as a benefit received from the royal family. Alexander’s account of dependency and advancement prepares his claim to loyalty.',devices:[{name:'Make transformation visible · antithesis',quote:'Instead of being subjects of the peoples who had plundered you and your property, you became their rulers.',mechanism:'The opposed roles compress the claimed transformation into a memorable reversal of power.',tryIt:'Describe a real change with a clear before and after. Show the evidence behind the contrast.'}]},
  {part:3,title:'List what Alexander claims to have given them',text:'Those were my father Philip’s achievements for you: great in themselves, but small beside what you have received from me. I inherited a few gold and silver cups and less than sixty talents in the treasury. Philip left debts of five hundred talents, and I borrowed another eight hundred. From a country that could barely support you, I opened the crossing of the Hellespont, even though Persia controlled the sea.\n\nWith my cavalry I defeated Darius’s governors. I added all Ionia and Aeolis, both Phrygias, and Lydia to your rule, and took Miletus by siege. The other places surrendered willingly, and I allowed you to take their wealth.\n\nThe riches of Egypt and Cyrene, won without a battle, came to you. Coele-Syria, Palestine, and Mesopotamia are yours. Babylon, Bactra, and Susa are yours. The wealth of Lydia, the treasures of Persia, the riches of India, and the outer sea are yours. You are governors, you are generals, you are captains.',explanation:'The expanding list makes the debt of gratitude feel large. Repeatedly assigning the gains to “you” prepares the contrast with what Alexander says he kept.',devices:[{name:'Let every gain return to the audience · epistrophe',quote:'Babylon, Bactra, and Susa are yours.',mechanism:'Successive sentences end with “are yours,” turning a list of places into a repeated claim about who benefited.',tryIt:'List three outcomes and end each sentence with the same beneficiary.'},{name:'Repeat their new identity · anaphora',quote:'You are governors, you are generals, you are captains.',mechanism:'The repeated opening makes each rank another reminder of the audience’s elevated position.',tryIt:'Repeat “You are” across three accurate descriptions of a group’s responsibilities.'}]},
  {part:4,title:'Answer the charge that only the king benefits',text:'What have I kept for myself after all this work, apart from this purple robe and crown? I have taken nothing for myself. No one can point to my treasures except what belongs to you or what I guard for you. I have no private reason to guard it: I eat the same food as you and take no more sleep. Indeed, I doubt that I eat as well as those of you who live in luxury. I know I often stay awake watching over you so that you can sleep.',explanation:'He contrasts the soldiers’ gains with his claimed lack of private reward. Shared food and lost sleep make his defense concrete, though the speech itself cannot verify these claims.',devices:[{name:'Ask a question that carries its answer · rhetorical question',quote:'What have I kept for myself after all this work, apart from this purple robe and crown?',mechanism:'The question presents his reward as small before the audience can formulate an answer.',tryIt:'Write a question that highlights a genuine imbalance, then check that you have not hidden contrary evidence.'}]},
  {part:4,title:'Make his wounds the evidence',text:'Perhaps someone will say that you did the hard work while I gained everything as your leader without sharing it. But who among you can say he has worked harder for me than I have for him? Come: anyone who has wounds, show them, and I will show mine. No part of the front of my body is free from wounds. There is no weapon used in close combat or thrown from a distance whose mark I do not carry.\n\nI have been wounded by swords in close fighting, struck by arrows and missiles from siege engines, and hit by stones and wooden bolts for your lives, your glory, and your wealth. Yet I still lead you as victors over land and sea, rivers, mountains, and plains.',explanation:'He states the objection and answers it with physical evidence. The invitation to compare wounds makes the audience judge his participation rather than only his authority.',devices:[{name:'Bring the objection into the open · anticipation and rebuttal',quote:'Perhaps someone will say that you did the hard work while I gained everything as your leader without sharing it.',mechanism:'Stating the opposing case allows him to choose a concrete answer: the injuries on his body.',tryIt:'State a serious criticism of your position in one sentence. Answer it with evidence the audience can examine.'}]},
  {part:3,title:'Recall care for soldiers and their families',text:'I celebrated your weddings alongside my own, and many of your children will be related to mine. I paid the debts of everyone who owed money, without asking closely how they incurred them, although you receive high pay and take much plunder after a siege. Most of you have gold crowns, lasting reminders of your courage and the honor I have given you.\n\nThose who died met a glorious end and received splendid burials. Bronze statues of many of the fallen stand at home. Their parents are honored and freed from public duties and taxes. None of you has ever died fleeing under my command.',explanation:'Money, recognition, family ties, and honors for the dead broaden the claim that his relationship with the soldiers goes beyond military usefulness.'},
  {part:5,title:'Make them imagine explaining their departure',text:'I intended to send home those no longer fit for service, so well rewarded that the people there would envy them. But since you all want to leave, all of you may go!\n\nGo home and report that your king Alexander conquered the Persians, Medes, Bactrians, and Sacae; subdued the Uxians, Arachotians, and Drangians; and ruled the Parthians, Chorasmians, and Hyrcanians as far as the Caspian Sea. Tell them he crossed the Caucasus and the Caspian Gates, the Oxus and Tanais, and the Indus, which no one but Dionysus had crossed before.\n\nTell them he crossed the Hydaspes, Acesines, and Hydraotes, and would have crossed the Hyphasis too if you had not drawn back in fear. He reached the great sea through both mouths of the Indus and marched through the desert of Gedrosia, where no army had marched before. On the way he took Carmania and the land of the Oritians, while his fleet sailed the coast from India to Persia.\n\nReport that, after bringing him back to Susa, you deserted him and left him under the protection of the foreigners he had conquered. Perhaps that story will bring you glory among men and credit with the gods. Go!”',explanation:'The long record of conquest ends in an imagined report of desertion. The sarcastic promise of glory asks soldiers to see themselves through the judgment of people at home. The geographical and heroic claims are the speaker’s claims in Arrian’s account.',devices:[{name:'Make praise mean blame · irony',quote:'Perhaps that story will bring you glory among men and credit with the gods.',mechanism:'The surrounding accusation makes “glory” mean its opposite. The speaker turns the audience’s concern for reputation against departure.',tryIt:'Rewrite an ironic accusation as a direct statement. Compare the force and the risk of alienating your listener.'}]}
 ]
};
